<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guanzhong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guanzhong is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a fertile basin enclosed by mountains and guarded passes. Its strategic strength made it the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> state and the seat of the capitals of Western Han, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> divided it among three former Qin generals, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-qins">Three Qins</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guanzhong.jpg" alt="Guanzhong" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ulamm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shudao.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guanzhong is a crescent-shaped basin in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, bounded by the Qinling Mountains to the south and by the Huanglong Mountain, Meridian Ridge and Long Mountain ranges to the north. Its central flatland, the Guanzhong or Wei River Plain, is made of alluvial plains along the lower Wei River and its tributaries, at 300 to 700 metres above sea level. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, the capital of Shaanxi, lies at its centre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, Tongchuan and Weinan are its other main cities.</p><p>The name means within the passes, after the fortresses that guarded it: Hangu Pass to the east, Wu Pass to the south-east, Xiao Pass to the north-west and Dasan Pass to the west, later joined by Tong Pass, built by the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, and Jinsuo Pass, built under the Tang. Hangu Pass, on a narrow corridor along the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, was long the only passage into the region from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, and its strength helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> defeat the alliances of its eastern rivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Guanzhong became the heartland of the Zhou after the Jī clan leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gugong-danfu">Gugong Danfu</a> led his people south from Bin to escape nomad raids, and from there the Zhou conquered the Shang in 1046 BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> sacked the Zhou capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a> in 771 BCE and the court fled east, the Yíng clan, rewarded for escorting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-ping-of-zhou">King Ping of Zhou</a>, built up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> state in the basin and moved its capital east by stages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>. Irrigation works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengguo-canal">Zhengguo Canal</a> made the region highly productive, and Qin unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE.</p><p>In 206 BCE the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> entered Guanzhong and received the surrender of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a>, but had to yield it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>, who sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>. Four months later Liu Bang returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a>, retook the region and used it as his base against Xiang Yu, and as founder of the Han he built a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, across the Wei from ruined Xianyang. Guanzhong was the capital region of twelve dynasties. By the Tang the economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had shifted south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> basin, and after the destruction of Chang'an at the end of the Tang, Guanzhong declined politically and economically.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guanzhong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Liu Mengzhi died, Liu Yu returned to Jiankang to secure his power, and the state of Xia reoccupied <b>Guanzhong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 359 BCE and his execution in 338 BCE the statesman Shang Yang introduced the reforms that made Qin a centralised state with a large army, and helped build the capital at Xianyang on the Wei River, in the <b>Guanzhong</b> heartland of fertile farmland protected by mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital Daxing lay in the secure <b>Guanzhong</b> but far from the economic centres to the east and south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Guanzhong</b> Plain stone axes developed in many forms, and by the late period some were made for woodworking and others for chopping, from different raw materials that suggest different groups made and used them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yangshao-culture">Yangshao culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom held the strategic Luoyang basin, the gateway between the <b>Guanzhong</b> region and the eastern plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-henan">Kingdom of Henan</a></li><li><blockquote>Xiang Yu divided the <b>Guanzhong</b> region among three former Qin commanders to block Liu Bang, giving Sima Xin the kingdom of Sai east of Xianyang, with its capital at Yueyang.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sai">Kingdom of Sai</a></li></ul></section>
